{{Nihongo|'''Takeru Fujimoto'''|藤本 健|Fujimoto Takeru; born February 24, 1977}} is a Sanese former politician and former professional [[w:Rugby union|rugby union]] player who served as the [[Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology (Okaiken)|Minister of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology]] from November 2025 to May 2026. A member of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]], he represented the [[Kagami Prefecture|Kagami]] 2nd district in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken]] from 2021 until his electoral defeat in the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|2026 general election]].

Widely regarded as one of the greatest rugby players in Okaiken's history, Fujimoto captained both the [[Aizawa Eagles]] at club level and the [[Okaiken national rugby union team|national team]] internationally. His physical, relentless playing style and leadership on the pitch made him an icon of the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} sporting philosophy. Following his retirement from professional rugby in 2010, he transitioned into education and later politics, becoming known for his "Martial Educator" persona.

==Early life==
[[File:Takeru_Fujimoto_Childhood.png|thumb|right|200px|Fujimoto as a child in Aizawa, circa 1985.]]
Fujimoto was born in [[Aizawa]], a major industrial city known for its shipyards and rugged coastal climate. The third of four sons in a working-class family, he was raised in the densely packed industrial districts where physical labor was a way of life. His father worked as a welder at the Aizawa Shipbuilding yards, while his mother managed a local izakaya.

From an early age, Fujimoto displayed exceptional physical coordination and an almost obsessive drive to improve. He began playing rugby at Aizawa Prefectural High School, where his aggressive tackling and tireless work rate quickly drew attention from regional scouts. By his third year, he had led the school team to the Aizawa Prefectural Championship and was named the tournament's Most Valuable Player.

==University career==
[[File:Takeru_Fujimoto_University.png|thumb|left|200px|Fujimoto during his Aizawa University rugby career, 1998.]]
Fujimoto attended [[Aizawa University]] on a rugby scholarship from 1995 to 1999, majoring in Physical Education. During his university years, he developed the punishing training regimen and leadership philosophy that would define his professional career. As captain in his final two years, he transformed the Aizawa University rugby club from a regional contender into a national powerhouse.

In the 1998 National University Rugby Championship, Fujimoto delivered a legendary performance in the final against [[Okami Teikoku University]]. Playing with a fractured rib sustained in the semifinal, he made 28 tackles and scored a try in the 78th minute to secure a dramatic 24–21 victory. The match is still replayed in rugby clinics across Okaiken as a masterclass in endurance and willpower.

==Professional rugby career==
===Club career: Aizawa Eagles (1999–2010)===
[[File:Aizawa_Eagles_Rugby.png|thumb|right|250px|Fujimoto during his tenure as captain of the [[Aizawa Eagles]], circa 2008.]]
Fujimoto signed with the [[Aizawa Eagles]] of the [[Okaiken Rugby League One|Top League]] in 1999, declining offers from larger corporate teams in Okami and Toyozawa to remain in his hometown. He made his debut on 12 September 1999 against the [[Koshien Steelers]], immediately establishing himself as a starting number eight.

His breakout season came in 2002–03, when he was named the Top League's {{nihongo|"Rookie of the Year"|新人王|Shinjin-ō}} despite being in his fourth professional season—the award recognized his transformation into the league's most dominant forward. That season, he recorded a league-high 187 tackles and scored 12 tries, including a hat-trick against the [[Saitama Wild Knights|Toshiba Brave Lupus]].

In 2005, Fujimoto was appointed captain of the Aizawa Eagles, a position he would hold until his retirement. Under his leadership, the Eagles adopted an intensely physical playing style that mirrored his own philosophy: relentless defensive pressure, brutal set-piece dominance, and unwavering collective sacrifice.

====2007–08: Championship season====
The 2007–08 season represented the pinnacle of Fujimoto's club career. Having lost in the Emperor's Shield final the previous two years, Fujimoto spent the offseason developing a revolutionary conditioning program that emphasized explosive power and extreme cardiovascular endurance—training methods that would later influence the [[National Iron Body Initiative]].

In the Emperor's Shield final against the heavily favored Saitama Wild Knights, Fujimoto played what is widely considered the greatest individual performance in Top League history. Despite suffering a separated shoulder in the 23rd minute, he remained on the pitch for the full 80 minutes, making 34 tackles and scoring the match-winning try in extra time. The Eagles won 28–25, claiming their first national championship in franchise history.

Fujimoto's final appearance for the Eagles came on 27 March 2010, a testimonial match at Aizawa Stadium that drew 42,000 spectators. He retired as the franchise's all-time leader in appearances (176), tries (37), and tackles (2,847).

===International career (2002–2010)===
[[File:Okaiken_National_Rugby_Team.png|thumb|left|250px|Fujimoto leading the [[Okaiken national rugby union team|Brave Blossoms]] onto the pitch during the 2007 Rugby World Cup.]]
Fujimoto made his debut for the [[Okaiken national rugby union team|Brave Blossoms]] on 8 June 2002 against [[Fenia|Fenian]] opposition in the Shendan Cup, a regional test series between Fosian rugby nations. He scored a try in the 64th minute of his debut match, which Okaiken won 31–17.

====Appointment as captain====
Following the 2005 Rugby World Cup qualifiers, head coach [[Kenshiro Takeshita]] appointed Fujimoto as national team captain, succeeding the retiring legend [[Masahiro Goto]]. Takeshita cited Fujimoto's "unbreakable spirit and ability to inspire collective resilience" as the deciding factors.

====2007 Rugby World Cup====
[[File:Fujimoto_2007_World_Cup.png|thumb|right|250px|Fujimoto during Okaiken's historic victory over Mercia at the 2007 Rugby World Cup.]]
Fujimoto captained Okaiken at the [[w:2007 Rugby World Cup|2007 Rugby World Cup]], where the Brave Blossoms achieved their best World Cup finish to date. In a historic pool stage match against [[Mercia]]—then ranked sixth in the world—Fujimoto delivered a man-of-the-match performance, making 22 tackles and scoring a try in a shocking 26–23 victory. The upset is still celebrated in Okaiken as a defining moment in the nation's rugby history.

The Brave Blossoms advanced to the quarter-finals for the first time, where they fell to eventual champions [[Volisania]] in a hard-fought 28–17 contest. Fujimoto was named to the World Cup Team of the Tournament at number eight, becoming the first Sanese player to receive the honor.

====2009 Shendan Cup triumph====
[[File:Fujimoto_Shendan_Cup_2009.png|thumb|left|250px|Fujimoto lifting the Shendan Cup trophy after Okaiken's 2009 victory.]]
In the 2009 Shendan Cup, Fujimoto led Okaiken to their first regional championship since 1991. In the final against [[Nakamizu]], he played through a torn calf muscle sustained in the semifinal, contributing 18 tackles and a crucial turnover in the final five minutes to preserve a 19–15 victory.

====Retirement from international rugby====
On 15 November 2010, Fujimoto announced his retirement from all forms of professional rugby, citing a desire to "build strength in the next generation." He finished his international career with 78 caps, 15 tries, and the distinction of being Okaiken's most successful captain of the modern era.

===Playing style and legacy===
Fujimoto played primarily as a [[w:Number eight (rugby union)|number eight]], though he was also deployed at [[w:Flanker|blindside flanker]] during his international career. At {{convert|1.88|m|ftin|abbr=on}} and {{convert|108|kg|stlb|abbr=on}}, he combined exceptional size with explosive acceleration and tireless work rate.

His playing style was characterized by:
* '''Relentless tackling:''' Fujimoto's defensive technique emphasized powerful, driving hits that stopped ball-carriers behind the gain line. He recorded over 200 tackles in a single season four times during his career.
* '''Ball-carrying ferocity:''' He was known for his ability to break through multiple defenders, often requiring three or four opponents to bring him down.
* '''Set-piece dominance:''' As a number eight, Fujimoto controlled the base of the scrum with exceptional technique, frequently winning strikes against the head.
* '''Leadership under pressure:''' Teammates and coaches consistently praised his ability to remain calm and make decisive calls in crucial moments.

[[Haruma Date]], current captain of the [[Saitama Wild Knights]] and the national team, has cited Fujimoto as his primary inspiration: "He taught us that rugby is not just about physical strength—it is about the iron spirit that refuses to break when everything tells you to fall."

