==Educational career==
Following his retirement from professional rugby in 2010, Fujimoto pursued a career in education, motivated by what he described as a desire to "strengthen the spirit of the next generation." He completed a teaching certification program at Aizawa University and began working as a physical education instructor at his alma mater, Aizawa Prefectural High School.

From 2011 to 2015, Fujimoto also served as the forwards coach for the [[Aizawa Eagles]], helping develop the next generation of Sanese rugby talent. Under his guidance, the Eagles reached two consecutive Emperor's Shield finals.

===Media appearances===
During this period, Fujimoto became a household name among elementary students after providing the voice for "Takeru-sensei," a central character in the national Trent language textbook series used for 3rd and 4th-grade students. His booming, authoritative, yet encouraging voice was credited with making language learning more engaging for young boys, and the character became synonymous with disciplined, encouraging instruction.

==Political career==
{{main|Political positions of Takeru Fujimoto}}
Fujimoto entered politics in 2021, successfully running for the [[Kagami Prefecture|Kagami]] 2nd district seat as a member of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]]. He aligned himself with the party's "Strength and Science" wing, which advocates for technological modernization paired with traditional Sanese values of discipline and collective responsibility.

===Minister of Education===
[[File:Fujimoto_Minister_Portrait.png|thumb|right|200px|Fujimoto as Minister of Education, November 2025.]]
In November 2025, Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]] appointed Fujimoto as the Minister of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology. His appointment was seen as a move to appeal to the "National Vigor" sentiment rising in Okaiken, particularly among younger voters drawn to his athletic legacy and no-nonsense public persona.

====The "Forged-Iron" Program====
Fujimoto's signature policy is the '''"Forged-Iron" Education Program''' ({{nihongo|Rentetsu-kyōiku|錬鉄教育}}). Drawing heavily from his rugby background and the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} philosophy, the program emphasizes three core pillars:
* '''Mandatory Participation:''' Requiring students to engage in intensive group activities, whether in sports or the arts, to prevent social withdrawal.
* '''Physical and Mental Strength:''' Integrating resilience training and rigorous physical standards into the standard curriculum.
* '''Radical Teamwork:''' Grading systems that reward collective achievement over individual competition, aimed at fostering national unity.

Critics from the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|Socialist Party]] have expressed concerns regarding the program's perceived "martial" undertones, but Fujimoto has remained steadfast, famously stating during a Diet debate: {{bq|A soft education produces a brittle nation. Just as iron must be struck repeatedly in the forge to become steel, our children must be challenged repeatedly to become strong. We do not build resilience by removing obstacles—we build it by teaching them to overcome obstacles together.}}

===Electoral defeat (2026)===
{{main|Okaiken general election, 2026}}
[[File:2026_Sanese_Election_Map.svg|thumb|right|300px|The 2026 general election produced the most dramatic realignment in modern Sanese political history, with the PPO collapsing from 181 to 63 seats.]]
Fujimoto lost his seat in the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|61st General Election]] on 17 May 2026, defeated by SCP challenger {{nihongo|Sano Yasuhiro|佐野康博}} in the Kagami-2nd constituency. The loss was part of a broader collapse of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|Progressive Party]], which saw its representation plummet from 181 seats to just 63—the party's worst electoral result since its founding.

Fujimoto's defeat was particularly notable given that Kagami-2nd had returned PPO members for eighteen consecutive years. Political analysts attributed his loss to a combination of factors:
* '''The Satori Blackout:''' As Minister of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology, Fujimoto bore responsibility for the science and technology portfolio during the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of May 1–4, 2026, which paralyzed the nation for 72 hours and claimed 23 lives.
* '''The "Forged-Iron" controversy:''' Critics argued that Fujimoto's national resilience doctrine, while rhetorically powerful, had focused on physical education and sports infrastructure at the expense of digital infrastructure resilience—the very systems that failed during the Blackout.
* '''The Ikazaki Effect:''' The late surge of the [[Sanese Communist Party]] under [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] disproportionately affected PPO incumbents in constituencies where left-wing voters had previously split between the PPO and SCP.

Fujimoto conceded defeat at 22:47 on election night, telling supporters: "The people of Kagami have spoken. Their verdict is clear. I accept it without reservation." He thanked his campaign staff and declined to make a statement about his political future.

===Post-political career===
[[File:Fujimoto_Youth_Coaching.png|thumb|left|250px|Fujimoto coaching youth rugby in Aizawa, 2027.]]
Following his electoral defeat, Fujimoto announced his retirement from active politics. In a statement issued on May 20, 2026—the same day the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]] was sworn in—Fujimoto said: "I entered politics to strengthen the next generation. That work continues, but it will not continue through me. The forge is closed. The iron is cast."

Fujimoto returned to Aizawa, where he has resumed his involvement in youth rugby development. In September 2026, he was appointed as a special advisor to the [[Aizawa Eagles]] youth academy, working with under-18 players from industrial districts across Aizawa Prefecture.

He has remained a vocal advocate for the "Forged-Iron" educational philosophy outside of government, publishing a series of essays in the ''Aizawa Mainichi'' on the relationship between physical resilience and civic character. However, these writings have attracted criticism from commentators who argue that the Satori Blackout exposed the gap between his rhetoric of "national resilience" and the digital vulnerabilities that developed under his party's governance.

====Legacy assessment====
Fujimoto's political legacy remains contested. Supporters point to his "Forged-Iron" program's emphasis on collective responsibility and physical education reform as genuine attempts to address Okaiken's social withdrawal crisis. The [[National Iron Body Initiative]], launched by [[Crown Prince Tomohito]], drew significant inspiration from Fujimoto's educational philosophy.

Critics, however, argue that Fujimoto's tenure exemplified the broader failures of the Honda government's "digital-first" modernization agenda. The disconnect between his advocacy for physical resilience and the digital infrastructure vulnerabilities that culminated in the Satori Blackout has been described by political scientist {{nihongo|Yamamoto Keiko|山本恵子}} of [[Okami University]] as "the central irony of the Fujimoto era—a minister of iron bodies overseeing a nation of brittle systems."

==Personal life==
Fujimoto married his university sweetheart, [[Yuki Matsuda]], in 2001. The couple have three children: sons [[Kenji Fujimoto|Kenji]] (born 2003) and [[Ryo Fujimoto|Ryo]] (born 2006), and daughter [[Mika Fujimoto|Mika]] (born 2009). Both sons have followed their father into rugby; Kenji currently plays as a flanker for the Aizawa University rugby club.

Fujimoto maintains a strict personal fitness regimen, waking at 5:00 AM daily for weight training and running. He is often seen practicing traditional Sanese calligraphy in his limited free time, citing the discipline required for brushwork as complementary to physical training. He continues to be a vocal advocate for rugby at the grassroots level and frequently visits school clubs in his district to personally coach student athletes.

